✂️ Split an Array
Definition

Splitting an array means dividing one array into two or more smaller arrays.

Example
Original Array:
[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]


Split into two parts:


[10, 20] | [30, 40, 50]
Method 1: Using Slicing

Python slicing makes splitting an array simple.

arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]


first_part = arr[:2]
second_part = arr[2:]


print(first_part)
print(second_part)
Output
[10, 20]
[30, 40, 50]
Explanation
arr[:2]

Takes elements from index 0 up to index 2 (not including 2).

arr[2:]

Takes elements from index 2 to the end.

Splitting at a Specific Index
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]


index = 3


left = arr[:index]
right = arr[index:]


print(left)
print(right)
Output
[10, 20, 30]
[40, 50]

Visual representation:

[10, 20, 30 | 40, 50]
          ↑
       Split here
Method 2: Using a Loop

We can manually split an array using a loop.

arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]


index = 2


first_part = []
second_part = []


for i in range(len(arr)):
    if i < index:
        first_part.append(arr[i])
    else:
        second_part.append(arr[i])


print(first_part)
print(second_part)
Output
[10, 20]
[30, 40, 50]
Splitting an Array into Equal Parts

Suppose we have:

[10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]

We want to split it into two equal parts.

arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60]


mid = len(arr) // 2


first_half = arr[:mid]
second_half = arr[mid:]


print(first_half)
print(second_half)
Output
[10, 20, 30]
[40, 50, 60]
Splitting an Array with an Odd Number of Elements
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]


mid = len(arr) // 2


first_half = arr[:mid]
second_half = arr[mid:]


print(first_half)
print(second_half)
Output
[10, 20]
[30, 40, 50]

The second part contains the extra element.

Time and Space Complexity

For an array with n elements:

Operation	Time Complexity	Extra Space
Split using slicing	O(n)	O(n)
Split using a loop	O(n)	O(n)

New arrays are created, so extra space is required.

Key Takeaways
Splitting divides an array into smaller parts.
Python slicing provides a simple way to split arrays.
arr[:index] gives elements before the split index.
arr[index:] gives elements from the split index onward.
Splitting an array generally takes O(n) time and O(n) extra space.
